📅 Day 21 – Java I/O Streams

Full Stack Web Development with Java Internship

Today’s session focused on Java I/O Streams and their role in handling input and output operations in Java programs. The mentor explained how streams allow data to flow between a program and external sources such as files.

🔹 Topics Covered
1️⃣ Introduction to I/O Streams

I/O stands for Input and Output.

Streams are used to read data from input sources and write data to output destinations.

Common sources include files, keyboard input, and network connections.

2️⃣ Types of Streams
✅ Byte Streams

Used for handling binary data such as images, audio, and video files.

Works with 8-bit bytes.

Examples:

FileInputStream

FileOutputStream

✅ Character Streams

Used for handling character-based data such as text files.

Works with 16-bit Unicode characters.

Examples:

FileReader

FileWriter

BufferedReader

BufferedWriter

3️⃣ File Handling Classes

The mentor introduced several important classes used for file operations:

FileInputStream → Reads data from a file

FileOutputStream → Writes data to a file

BufferedReader → Efficient reading of text data

BufferedWriter → Efficient writing of text data

🔹 Practical Implementation

Wrote programs to read data from a file using input streams.

Implemented programs to write output into a file.

Practiced using buffered streams for better performance.